BCC_Q({"lv": "910", "y": 2023, "n": 14, "part": "C", "topic": "graph", "title": "Watercolour", "answer": 3, "story": "<p>A beaver designs mazes on rectangular grids of squares. To make the\nmazes more interesting, it can pour watercolour on a square. The colour\nthen spreads. Every second, colour reaches each uncoloured square that\nshares an edge with a coloured square. However, colour does not spread\nthrough walls. Here is an example:<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"A grid with 3 rows and 4 columns. There is a vertical wall along the right sides of the bottom two squares in the first column and a horizontal wall along the top sides of the two middle squares in the bottom row. The second square in the middle row is coloured and the remaining squares are uncoloured.\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:230px\"/> <img alt=\"The second square in the top row and the second and third squares in the middle row are coloured.\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:230px\"/> <img alt=\"The first three squares in the top row and the last three squares in the middle row are coloured.\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:230px\"/> <img alt=\"All squares in the top and middle rows are coloured, as is the last square in the bottom row. There are three uncoloured squares remaining.\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:230px\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>If different colours are used and poured into two different squares,\nthen the first colour that spreads to an uncoloured square will fill it\ncompletely and no new colour will be added. If two colours reach a\nsquare at the same time, the square takes the darker colour.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"The same 3 by 4 grid with a vertical wall and a horizontal wall. The first square in the top row is coloured dark blue. The last square in the middle row is coloured light pink. The remaining squares are uncoloured.\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:230px\"/> <img alt=\"The first two squares in the top row and the first square in the middle row are dark blue. The last square in the top row, the last two squares in the middle row, and the last square in the bottom row are light pink.\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:230px\"/> <img alt=\"The first three squares in the top row, the first two squares in the middle row, and the first square in the bottom row are dark blue. The last square in the top row, the last two squares in the middle row, and the last two squares in the bottom row are light pink.\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:230px\"/> <img alt=\"There are no new dark blue squares. The second square in the bottom row is now light pink and there are no uncoloured squares remaining.\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:230px\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>", "question": "<p>If different colours are poured as shown, what will the maze look\nlike when the maze is filled with colour?<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p> <img alt=\"An alternative format for the diagram\nfollows.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:30.0%\"/> <\/p>\n<\/div>\n<button onclick=\"hideSeek('longdesc7')\" style=\"box-shadow: none;\">Hide/Reveal Alternative Format for Watercolour\nQuestion<\/button>\n<div id=\"longdesc7\" style=\"display: none;\">\n<p>The maze is a grid with 6 rows and 8 columns. The square in row 2 and\ncolumn 6 is dark blue. The square in row 5 and column 2 is light pink.\nThe outer edges of the grid have walls and many squares have walls along\none or more of their sides inside the grid.<\/p>\n<p>The following table represents the maze, providing the locations of\nthe walls. If a square in the maze has a wall along one or more of its\nsides, then this is indicated in the corresponding cell in the table.\nFor example, the square in row 1 and column 1 has an exterior wall along\nits left side and its top side and so the cell in row 1 and column 1 has\nentry \"left, top\" and the the square in row 2 and column 1 has an\nexterior wall along its left side and an interior wall along its bottom\nside, so the cell in row 2 and column 1 has entry \"left, bottom\".<\/p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">left, top<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">top<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">top<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">top<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">top<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">top<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">top<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">top, right<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">left, bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">left, bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">right<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">left, top<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">top, right<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">left, top, bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">right<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">left, top<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">top<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">right<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">left<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">top, bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">top, bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">right<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">left, right<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">left<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">top<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">top<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">right<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">left, top<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">top<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">right<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">left, right, bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">left, bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">right, bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">left, bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">bottom<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">right, bottom<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<hr class=\"longdesc\"/><\/div>\n<ol class=\"mc\">\n<li><br/><img alt=\"21 squares are coloured light pink and the remaining 27 are dark blue. The following squares are pink: the first five squares in each of rows 3, 4, 5, and 6, along with the sixth square in row 4.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:30.0%\"/> <\/li>\n<li><br/><img alt=\"21 squares are coloured light pink and the remaining 27 are dark blue. The following squares are pink: the first five squares in each of rows 4, 5, and 6, along with the first, second, fifth, and sixth squares in row 3 and the sixth and seventh squares in row 4.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:30.0%\"/> <\/li>\n<li><br/><img alt=\"26 squares are coloured light pink and the remaining 22 are dark blue. The following squares are pink: all squares in rows 4, 5 and 6, along with the first two squares in row 3.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:30.0%\"/> <\/li>\n<li><br/><img alt=\"18 squares are coloured light pink and the remaining 30 are dark blue. The following squares are pink: the first five squares in each of rows 4, 5, and 6, along with the first two squares in row 3 and the sixth square in row 4.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:30.0%\"/> <\/li>\n<\/ol>", "answerHtml": "<p>(D)<br/><img alt=\"18 squares are coloured light pink and the remaining 30 are dark blue. The following squares are pink: the first five squares in each of rows 4, 5, and 6, along with the first two squares in row 3 and the sixth square in row 4.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:30.0%\"/> <\/p>", "explanation": "<p>The image below shows the state of the maze second by second:<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p> <img alt=\"The number of dark blue\nand light pink squares in the maze grows gradually over 8 seconds. All\nsquares in the maze are coloured after 8 seconds as in Option D.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:100.0%\"/> <\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>Thus, when the maze is filled with colour it will look like the maze\nin Option D.<\/p>", "connections": "<p>In order to solve this task, we can use a technique called\n<em>breadth-first search<\/em>, and specifically, a variant called the\n<em>flood fill<\/em> algorithm.<\/p>\n<p>Breadth-first search is an <em>algorithm<\/em> that searches by\nlooking at all immediate \"next locations\" based on the \"current\nlocation\". In this task, the next locations are the interior spaces\nbehind an exterior wall which is being flooded. This process repeats,\nwith the most recently added \"next locations\" becoming the \"current\nlocation\". When observing the pictures used in the Explanation of\nAnswer, the entire area is increasingly filled as if it were being\nflooded from two locations, and thus, the algorithm is known as\n<em>flood fill<\/em>.<\/p>\n<p>One very common use of breadth-first search is in finding the\n<em>shortest path<\/em> between two given points, such as when finding\nthe best sequence of directions to drive from one location to another.\nIn this task, we are finding the shortest path from the initial square\nto the squares that are at the \"boundary\" between the two colours.<\/p>\n<p>Flood fill algorithms can be found in many graphical drawing programs\nwhich contain a \"bucket fill\" tool that will shade an entire region with\na certain colour: the algorithm will colour adjacent <em>pixels<\/em> the\nsame colour until it locates a boundary pixel of a different colour.<\/p>"});